    },
    {
      "id": "rn_XVWxCbPYwyZjNKFD::L1::Q8",
      "moduleId": "rn_XVWxCbPYwyZjNKFD::L1",
      "stem": "Explain why identical molecular formulae can still describe different compounds.",
      "misconceptionClass": "formula insufficiency logic",
      "kind": "explain-why",
      "modelAnswer": "A molecular formula only inventories atoms; how those atoms connect - which bond links which atom - is extra information, and different connectivities are genuinely different substances.",
      "keywords": [
        "formula",
        "connect",
        "different",
        "substances"
      ],
      "options": []
    },
    {
      "id": "rn_XVWxCbPYwyZjNKFD::L1::Q9",
      "moduleId": "rn_XVWxCbPYwyZjNKFD::L1",
      "stem": "A student lists two structures, both C3H6, one with a C=C and one as a triangle ring. State the isomer relationship and the differing feature.",
      "misconceptionClass": "pair identification application",
      "kind": "application",
      "modelAnswer": "They are structural isomers - propene and cyclopropane - sharing the C3H6 molecular formula while differing in structure: open chain versus ring.",
      "keywords": [
        "structural isomers",
